mainmenu "Project CLASS Multi-Function Sensor 1 (nRF54L05)"

config MFS_SCAN_PERIOD_MS
	int "Passive scan period (milliseconds)"
	default 6000
	help
	  How often the device wakes to scan. The default 6000 ms is preset 5 of
	  the ladder in docs/v1-scope.md section 7.1 and budgets at ~69 uA, about
	  2.4 years on a CR123A. The useful range is 1000-30000 ms; above 30000 ms
	  the cell's shelf life caps battery life and longer periods buy nothing.

config MFS_SCAN_WINDOW_MS
	int "Passive scan window (milliseconds)"
	default 100
	help
	  How long each scan listens. The counterpart is not a single 20-50 ms
	  advertising interval: the phone repeats the SAME command advert roughly
	  every 187 ms for a 30 s burst per Send (~160 repeats - see
	  docs/tan-scheme.md section 6, design spec section 3), so a 100 ms window
	  has many scan wakes across that burst to catch it, not just one.
	  Shortening this window trades detection reliability, not latency.

config MFS_ADXL_INACTIVITY_SECS
	int "ADXL367 loop inactivity time (seconds)"
	default 5
	help
	  Stillness required before the ADXL367 activity engine re-arms. This is
	  also the LED B timeout — the hardware loop does the timing, so there is
	  no software timer.

config MFS_ADXL_THRESHOLD
	int "ADXL367 referenced-activity threshold (13-bit) - UNUSED"
	default 300
	help
	  UNUSED. Grepping src/ and boards/ finds no reference to
	  CONFIG_MFS_ADXL_THRESHOLD at all - the threshold is entirely superseded
	  by the engineer-settable sensitivity byte (Settings::ThresholdLsb(),
	  default 143 -> 302 LSB, see src/settings.cpp), which every
	  ConfigureLoopMode() call now uses instead. This option is dead and kept
	  only for reference pending removal.

	  Deviation from the reference that counts as motion.

	  SCALE: 0.25 mg per LSB on the +/-2 g range, so 300 = 75 mg. The register
	  is 13-bit, giving a usable range of 1 to 8191 (0.25 mg to ~2 g).

	  alc_drawer_master uses 150 (37.5 mg) for a cupboard door. 300 was chosen
	  here as a less twitchy starting point for the anti-tamper role; tune it
	  against real handling of the installed product.

	  Referenced mode only — never Absolute, or the vertical gravity component
	  latches the part awake permanently. See docs/v1-scope.md section 3.

config MFS_ADXL_INACTIVITY_THRESHOLD
	int "ADXL367 referenced-inactivity threshold (13-bit)"
	default 8191
	help
	  MUST be wide enough to cover any orientation change, which is up to 2 g
	  (an axis swinging from +1 g to -1 g). 8191 is 13-bit full scale, ~2 g.

	  DO NOT set this near the activity threshold. The inactivity reference is
	  captured when ACTIVITY fires, and the datasheet is explicit that in linked
	  and looped mode "the inactivity threshold cannot be continuously updated
	  ... the accelerometer does not detect inactivity until the acceleration
	  input returns to within the inactivity threshold". Set the board down in a
	  different orientation and it never returns: AWAKE sticks permanently and
	  the device stops triggering, silently.

	  At full scale, inactivity becomes a genuine "N seconds since the last
	  activity" timer, which is the specified behaviour and is immune to
	  orientation.

config MFS_ADXL_ACTIVITY_SAMPLES
	int "ADXL367 sustained samples required to latch activity"
	default 5
	help
	  TIME_ACT, in ODR samples. At the configured 100 Hz ODR, 5 samples is
	  50 ms of sustained motion above the threshold before activity latches.

	  This is the transient-rejection knob and it is cheap: it discriminates a
	  knock, a slammed door or a passing lorry from someone actually handling
	  the device, at the cost of the same delay in detection latency.
	  alc_drawer_master uses 2 (20 ms); alc_flush_master uses 20 samples at
	  25 Hz (800 ms) to reject cistern fill-valve transients.

config MFS_BATTERY_TEST
	bool "Battery-life test build: LED A becomes a periodic liveness blink"
	default n
	help
	  Repurposes LED A from "on while Inactive" to a short blink once per scan
	  period, so an unattended device can be seen to be alive across a long
	  battery test. LED B is unchanged. The device still starts deactivated and
	  is still armed by the engineer toggle.

	  READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THE RESULT: the blink dominates the power
	  budget. At an assumed 2 mA of LED current, 500 ms every 6000 ms is 8.3%
	  duty, i.e. ~167 uA against a ~69 uA operating budget - so the measurement
	  becomes mostly a measurement of the LED. See docs/battery-test.md.

config MFS_BLINK_MS
	int "Liveness blink duration (milliseconds)"
	default 500
	depends on MFS_BATTERY_TEST
	help
	  Rounded down to the 100 ms main-loop poll interval.

	  SET TO 0 TO DISABLE LED A ENTIRELY - not blinking, and not lit as an
	  Inactive indicator either. That is the setting for a true measurement.
	  LED B still works, so arming the device and nudging it is the liveness
	  check.

	  500 ms is highly visible but costs ~167 uA at 2 mA LED current. 100 ms
	  costs ~33 uA and is still clearly visible; 10 ms would cost ~3 uA but
	  needs the loop poll shortened to resolve it. Lower this if the aim is to
	  measure the scan rather than the LED.

config MFS_SCAN_DIAG
	bool "Log scan reception counts and discarded command IDs (bench only)"
	default n
	help
	  Bench diagnostic for commands that are advertised but never acted on.
	  Once per second in which anything was received, logs how many adverts
	  and how many single 128-bit service UUIDs the scanner delivered; and for
	  every UUID the access check discards as not for this device, logs its
	  first four on-air bytes. Tells "not received" apart from "received and
	  silently discarded". Leave off in production - it reveals reception.

config MFS_DEBUG_LED
	bool "Drive the idle/detection indication on the LEDs (bench only)"
	default y
	help
	  Between acknowledgement patterns: LED A on while Inactive, LED B shows
	  DETECTION (the ADXL367 loop engine's 5 s period) rather than the fire
	  output - in EITHER arm state, so it simulates triggers while tuning
	  Inactive and confirms one while Active. NOT VIABLE IN PRODUCTION for
	  LED A: a continuously lit LED draws ~2 mA against a ~69 uA budget,
	  roughly 29 days of battery life. Set n for production until a bounded
	  indication scheme is chosen — see docs/v1-scope.md section 5.

	  Independent of this option, in EVERY build including production: LED A
	  still plays the acknowledgement pattern for an accepted command (Armed,
	  Disarmed, Settings applied, Mode changed, Arm refused, ...). Setting n
	  only removes the idle "on while Inactive" level between patterns.

config ALC_DEVICE_SERIAL
	string "Device serial number"
	default "MFS-0000-0000"
	help
	  Unique serial identifier (e.g. MFS-0826-0043). The prefix is
	  provisional pending assignment of the CLASS device type.

config MFS_DEVICE_ID
	string "Device ID (8-char hex string)"
	default ""
	help
	  Public 32-bit identity bound into every key derivation, e.g. 4D465331.
	  Not secret and never transmitted. The Network Manager looks the device
	  up by it. See docs/tan-scheme.md section 3.

config MFS_DEVICE_SECRET
	string "Device secret (64-char hex string)"
	default ""
	help
	  32-byte per-device secret from which every day key is derived.
	  Loaded from credentials.conf (gitignored) for bench work only.
	  PRODUCTION MUST USE THE nRF54L05 KMU - a secret in the image is
	  readable, and a readable secret yields every day key for this device
	  forever. See docs/tan-scheme.md section 8.

config MFS_PROVISION_KEY
	string "Provisioning key (64-char hex string)"
	default ""
	help
	  32-byte per-device key authorising a time sync while the clock is
	  invalid, and nothing else. Must differ from MFS_DEVICE_SECRET so that a
	  compromised provisioner can cause denial of service but not entry. The
	  firmware refuses to enable commands if the two are equal. See
	  docs/tan-scheme.md section 8.1.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
